TR(1)                          P32W Unix                           TR(1)

NAME
       tr - translate, delete or squeeze characters

SYNOPSIS
       tr [-cCs] string1 string2
       tr -s [-cC] string1
       tr -d [-cC] string1
       tr -ds [-cC] string1 string2

DESCRIPTION
       tr copies the standard input to the standard output, changing
       characters on the way. With two strings and no -d each
       character of string1 is replaced by the character in the same
       place in string2; if string2 is shorter it is padded with its
       last character. A character given twice in string1 takes the
       last mapping. Characters are bytes (0-255); NULs pass through.

       The strings may hold:

       c      an ordinary character
       \n \t \r \a \b \f \v \\
              newline, tab, carriage return, bell, backspace, form
              feed, vertical tab, backslash
       \NNN   the character with octal code NNN (1 to 3 digits)
       c-d    the characters c to d in order (an error if d comes
              before c); a - first or last is itself
       [:class:]
              the characters of class, in order: alpha digit alnum
              space upper lower punct print graph cntrl blank xdigit
       [=c=]  the character c
       [c*n]  n copies of c (n decimal, or octal if it starts with 0)
       [c*]   in string2 only: as many copies of c as make string2
              as long as string1 (also [c*0])

       [:lower:] and [:upper:] in the same places in the two strings
       change case.

OPTIONS
       Options may be combined (-cs) or given separately; -- ends
       them.

       -c, -C use the complement of string1: every character not in
              it, in ascending order
       -d     delete the characters of string1
       -s     squeeze: write only one of each run of a character
              that is in the last string given (string2 if there is
              one, after translating; else string1)

EXAMPLES
       tr a-z A-Z                    upper case
       tr '[:upper:]' '[:lower:]'    lower case
       tr -cs '[:alpha:]' '[\n*]'    one word to a line
       tr -d '\r' < dosfile          drop carriage returns
       tr -s ' '                     runs of spaces to one

EXIT STATUS
       0; 1 for a usage error or a bad string.

SEE ALSO
       cut(1), od(1)

BUGS
       There are no locales: classes are those of the C locale. Any
       class may be used in string2, where it stands for its
       characters in order.
